name: types and their edges
intro: C has no "number". It has types, and each one is a fixed count of bits
intro: that decides which values fit and what happens at the edge. Pick the
intro: wrong one and the program still compiles.
intro:
intro: The ones you will meet:
intro:
intro:     char      one byte: a character, or a small number
intro:     int       the natural size; 32 bits here; print with %d
intro:     unsigned  an int with no minus sign, 0 and up; print with %u
intro:     long      at least 32 bits; 64 on 64-bit Linux; print with %ld
intro:               (unsigned long: %lu)
intro:     long long at least 64 bits; print with %lld; write 5LL
intro:     double    a number with a fraction; print with %f
intro:     size_t    sizes and counts; unsigned; print it with %zu
intro:     bool      true or false; needs #include <stdbool.h>
intro:
intro: A variable is a type, a name and a starting value: `int x = 7;`.
intro: printf fills each % in its text with the values after it, in order.
intro: `%s` is for a string, text in double quotes. `%.2f` is a double with
intro: 2 decimals, rounded:
intro:
intro:     long n = 4;
intro:     printf("%ld of %s is %.2f\n", n, "pi", 3.14159);
intro:
intro: prints `4 of pi is 3.14`.
intro:
intro: `a > b` is a comparison: it gives 1 (true) or 0 (false), and a bool
intro: holds it. Others: `<` `>=` `<=` `==` (equal) `!=` (not equal). To
intro: turn true/false into words, use `cond ? one : two` -- it gives `one`
intro: when cond is true, else `two`:
intro:
intro:     printf("%s\n", n > 3 ? "big" : "small");
intro:
intro: prints `big`.
intro:
intro: `int` and `long` change size from machine to machine. When the size
intro: matters, name it exactly: put `#include <stdint.h>` at the top and use
intro: int8_t, int16_t, int32_t, int64_t -- the number is how many bits, and
intro: 8 bits make a byte. A u in front means unsigned, no minus sign:
intro: uint8_t holds 0 to 255.
intro:
intro: `sizeof` asks how many bytes a type takes. Write it like a call, with
intro: the type inside: sizeof(int) is 4 here, sizeof(char) is always 1. The
intro: number it gives back is a size_t, so printf shows it with %zu:
intro:
intro:     printf("int %zu\n", sizeof(int));
intro:
intro: prints `int 4`. The text before %zu is printed as-is; %zu is where the
intro: number goes.
intro:
intro: Printing the stdint types themselves takes the macros in
intro: <inttypes.h>, which glue onto the format string:
intro:
intro:     int64_t big = INT64_C(3000000000) * 3;
intro:     printf("%" PRId64 "\n", big);
intro:
intro: The name is PRI + d (signed) or u (unsigned) + the bits: PRId32,
intro: PRIu32, PRIu64. `INT64_C(3000000000)` makes the literal an int64_t
intro: on every machine, so the multiply is done in 64 bits even where
intro: long is only 32.
intro:
intro: The edges have names. <limits.h> has INT_MIN, INT_MAX, LONG_MIN and
intro: LONG_MAX. <stdint.h> has SIZE_MAX and INT32_MAX, INT32_MIN,
intro: UINT32_MAX, INT64_MAX, UINT64_MAX and so on.
intro:
intro: A literal `%` in a printf format is written `%%`.
intro:
intro: Signed and unsigned behave differently at the edge. Unsigned arithmetic
intro: wraps, by definition: 0u - 1 is the largest value. Signed overflow is
intro: undefined behaviour: the compiler may assume it never happens and
intro: delete your checks. Never rely on it, never test for it afterwards.
intro:
intro: `a % b` is the remainder of a / b: 7 % 3 is 1, and `n % k == 0`
intro: means n is a multiple of k.
intro:
intro: Integer division truncates toward zero, and `%` takes the sign of the
intro: left side: -7 / 2 is -3, -7 % 2 is -1. And 7 / 2 is 3, not 3.5 -- make
intro: one side a double first: (double)7 / 2.
intro:
intro: Small types are promoted to int before any arithmetic, so uint8_t + 10
intro: is an int and can be 260. Assigning it back wraps; a cast says you
intro: meant it:
intro:
intro:     uint8_t r = (uint8_t)(a + 10);
---
q: sizes.c -- how big is each type
spec: Print one line per type, the type name, a space, and its size in bytes,
spec: using `sizeof` and `%zu`. In this order:
spec:
spec: `int8_t`, `int16_t`, `int32_t`, `int64_t`, `char`
spec:
spec: For example the first line is `int8_t 1`.
file: sizes.c
prog: #include <stdint.h>
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: 
prog: int main(void)
prog: {
prog: 	printf("int8_t %zu\n", sizeof(int8_t));
prog: 	printf("int16_t %zu\n", sizeof(int16_t));
prog: 	printf("int32_t %zu\n", sizeof(int32_t));
prog: 	printf("int64_t %zu\n", sizeof(int64_t));
prog: 	printf("char %zu\n", sizeof(char));
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: sizes
case: sizes | tail -n 2
case: sizes; echo $?
hint: sizeof gives a size_t, so the format is %zu, not %d
